00:00:06.499 --> 00:00:08.780 align:start position:0%
representatives from EU Member States  taking part in the banking union have  signed an agreement on transferring  finances and sharing them in a common  fund to help out ailing banks the funds  part of the so called single resolution  mechanism which consists of a board of  members who decide how to wind down or  restructure banks that are no longer  viable  once regular financing options have been  exhausted the board could draw on the  single resolution fund which will  eventually total some 55 billion euros  this would help limit costs to average  European citizens and the real economy  no longer do the taxpayers bail out  failing banks but investors are bailed  in and that will change the rules of the  game significantly the European Central  Bank will act as a supervisor and early  warning system alerting the board if a  banks in trouble or likely to fail apart  from helping to ward off any looming  crisis  the mechanism is expected to boost  economic growth as confidence grows that  Europe's banks are all using the same  rulebook  it would also sidestep the risks of  contagion and avoid the problems  involved in having national authorities  wind down cross-border banks it's it's a  very easy system and you can compare it  actually to how you organize your  financial market domestically you have  one supervisor you have one set of rules  which stabilizes expectations of  investors of consumers of depositors in  practice it requires a large set of  rules which sometimes are mathematical  and sometimes organizational but the  principle itself as we show here is an  easy one the single resolution mechanism  will start going into operation on  January 1st it will apply to banks in  the eurozone and other EU member states  that choose to join the banking Union
 

